#!/usr/bin/env bash
# Emit a fresh, run-scoped scratch directory for one Codex review-gate
# invocation, off any protected path. The gate writes its transient files —
# the prompt files, the round ledger, and any handoff — inside the printed
# dir and reuses that one dir across every round of the same gate.
#
# Why a fresh dir per call (unlike sdd-dir, which is stable per repo): the
# gate is a bounded, self-contained loop inside a single skill step, not a
# resumable multi-session workflow, so it needs no cross-invocation
# stability. What it does need is that two review gates running at once never
# share a ledger or clobber each other's prompt files — even two Claude Code
# sessions in the SAME worktree, or the spec gate and code gate of one run.
# A stable per-repo dir (git-dir-keyed alone) would still collide in those
# cases; a per-invocation `mktemp -d` cannot. So the caller runs this once at
# gate start, captures the path, and reuses it for the whole gate.
#
# The git-dir-keyed parent is only for tidiness — it groups a repo's runs
# together and mirrors sdd-dir's layout. Isolation comes from mktemp, not the
# key.
#
# Field evidence this is a real bug, not a hypothetical: the prior flat path
# hard-coded one shared ledger, `.../codex-review/codex-round-ledger.md`, for
# every session on the machine. Two symptoms were observed in the wild. (1)
# Users had begun hand-prefixing ledger filenames per project (ddf-, lic-,
# oeio-cube-, ctx-, ...) to dodge the collision — a manual workaround for
# missing isolation. (2) A session reviewing the `oedesalt` plan pointed Codex
# at the bare shared ledger while that file still held a DIFFERENT project's
# (`oefp`) spec-review findings from three days earlier, so the re-review
# prompt asserted "this ledger DOES pertain to the files below" about a ledger
# that did not. A fresh per-invocation dir makes both impossible.
#
# Why not <git-dir>/... or ~/.claude/...: .git/ and Claude's home are
# hardcoded protected paths in Claude Code. Writes there prompt in every
# permission mode except bypassPermissions, and that check runs BEFORE
# permission rules and PreToolUse hooks — so neither an allow-glob nor a hook
# can suppress it. Living under the user cache keeps writes on the normal
# permission path, and outside the working tree so scratch is never committed.
# Hashing uses `git hash-object` rather than shasum/sha256sum because the
# latter's name differs across platforms while git is already a hard
# dependency here.
set -euo pipefail

git rev-parse --git-dir >/dev/null 2>&1 || {
  echo "codex-review-dir: not inside a git repository" >&2
  exit 2
}

key=$(printf '%s' "$(git rev-parse --absolute-git-dir)" | git hash-object --stdin)
base="${XDG_CACHE_HOME:-$HOME/.cache}/hyperpowers/codex-review/${key}"
mkdir -p "$base"
mktemp -d "${base}/run-XXXXXXXX"
